IMPORT prelude

§ `The shared vocabulary`

-- WHAT THIS FILE IS. Four jurisdictions decide the same question -- one party
-- contracted under a mistake, the other did not; is the contract good? -- and
-- they decide it differently. Comparing them is only possible if they are
-- asked about the SAME facts, so this module holds the facts and nothing
-- else. There is no rule of law in this file. Every rule lives in the
-- jurisdiction module that states it, and each of those imports this one.
--
-- The fields below are the union of what the four tests ask about. A field
-- that only one jurisdiction reads is still declared here, because a case
-- record that changed shape per jurisdiction could not be compared across
-- them, which is the whole point.

§§ `What the mistaken party was mistaken about`

-- Singapore, England and Australia all confine the doctrine to a mistake as
-- to a TERM of the contract. The United States does not: Restatement (Second)
-- of Contracts s 153 reaches a mistake as to "a basic assumption on which he
-- made the contract", which is wider. So the distinction has to be in the
-- facts, or the American rule cannot be stated at all.
DECLARE `Subject of the mistake` IS ONE OF
    `a term of the contract`
    `a basic assumption that is not a term`

§§ `What the other party knew`

-- The three rungs matter because the jurisdictions cut the ladder in
-- different places. Singapore's common law limb needs the top rung and
-- nothing less (Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] 1 SLR(R)
-- 502 at [53]); its equitable limb starts at the middle rung (at [80]); the
-- American rule reaches the middle rung on its own (s 153(b), "had reason to
-- know").
--
-- The rungs are cumulative: a party with actual knowledge necessarily has
-- reason to suspect. `at least suspected` below is what encodes that, and it
-- is why no rule should test the middle rung by equality.
DECLARE `State of knowledge` IS ONE OF
    `neither knowledge nor reason to suspect`
    `reason to suspect a mistake`
    `actual knowledge of the mistake`

§§ `What the other party did about what he knew`

-- Singapore's additional element of impropriety (Chwee Kin Keong at [80]) is
-- about CONDUCT, not about knowledge, and the Court of Appeal drew two lines
-- through it that this enum has to be able to represent:
--
--   * negligence is not enough -- "Negligence per se, on the other hand,
--     should not be sufficient to invoke equity. Parties to a contract do not
--     owe a duty of care to each other" (at [77]); and
--
--   * a conscious omission IS enough -- "a conscious omission to disabuse a
--     mistaken party is itself sufficient to constitute unconscionable
--     conduct" (at [73]), and "[t]he conduct of deliberately not bringing the
--     suspicion of a possible mistake to the attention of the mistaken party
--     could constitute such impropriety" (at [80]).
--
-- The fourth rung is Australia's, and it is stronger than Singapore's third:
-- Taylor v Johnson (1983) 151 CLR 422 asks whether the other party
-- "deliberately set out to ensure that the [mistaken] party does not become
-- aware of his mistake or misapprehension" (at 432, quoted in Chwee Kin Keong
-- at [73]). Keeping the two apart is what lets the comparison show that
-- Singapore intervenes on facts Australia's stated test does not reach.
DECLARE `Conduct towards the mistake` IS ONE OF
    `nothing beyond accepting the bargain`
    `carelessness alone`
    `a conscious omission to disabuse`
    `deliberate steps to prevent discovery`

§§ `The case`

DECLARE `Mistake case` HAS
    `the case`                                     IS A STRING
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about`   IS A `Subject of the mistake`
    `the mistake was fundamental`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `what the other party knew`                    IS A `State of knowledge`
    `what the other party did`                     IS A `Conduct towards the mistake`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake`          IS A BOOLEAN
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `enforcement would be unconscionable`                       IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `What becomes of the contract`

-- Void and voidable are not two words for the same thing, and the difference
-- is the practical bite of the whole comparison. A void contract never
-- existed, so nobody can take title through it; a voidable one is good until
-- the mistaken party rescinds, so a third party who buys in the meantime may
-- keep what he bought. The Court of Appeal had exactly this in mind when it
-- declined to collapse the two limbs into one: "we believe that simplicity
-- may not always lead to a just result, especially where innocent third
-- parties are involved" (Chwee Kin Keong at [76]).
DECLARE `What becomes of the contract` IS ONE OF
    `the contract stands`
    `the contract is void`
    `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`

§§ `Reading the knowledge ladder`

@ref The rungs are cumulative; actual knowledge entails reason to suspect.
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the other party actually knew` IF
    CONSIDER c's `what the other party knew`
    WHEN `actual knowledge of the mistake` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Constructive knowledge, in the sense equity uses: knowledge or reason to suspect.
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the other party at least had reason to suspect` IF
    CONSIDER c's `what the other party knew`
    WHEN `neither knowledge nor reason to suspect` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE
